INT. COUNTRY HOUSE LIBRARY - NIGHT

Leather, mahogany, a banked fire. A grandfather clock strikes eleven. Rain beats at the French windows.

INSPECTOR HORACE LANGLEY, 50s, trench coat draped over a chair, stands at the mantelpiece studying a small bronze statue. At the desk by the window sits LADY PRUDENCE WHITMORE, 60s, black dress, hands folded over a closed ledger. A telephone sits between them.

LANGLEY
A lovely piece.

PRUDENCE
My late husband's.

LANGLEY
He had taste.

PRUDENCE
He had money.

LANGLEY
They're not always the same.

She does not smile. He sets the statue down and turns it a quarter inch, so its face looks toward the door.

LANGLEY (CONT'D)
The butler says he found the body at seven.

PRUDENCE
He did.

LANGLEY
And you were?

PRUDENCE
In the conservatory. Watering the orchids.

LANGLEY
At seven in the evening.

PRUDENCE
Orchids keep their own hours.

The telephone rings. Both look at it. It rings again. Prudence lifts the receiver, listens, and holds it out to Langley.

PRUDENCE (CONT'D)
For you.

Langley takes the receiver and carries it, on its long cord, out through the door into the hall. Prudence listens. She cannot see him, and his voice reaches her only in pieces.

LANGLEY (O.S.)
Langley. Yes. The gardener. He saw whom?

A pause.

LANGLEY (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Lady Whitmore. In the garden, at half past six. Carrying a spade. Thank you, Sergeant.

The receiver is replaced. He returns, trailing the cord, and sets the telephone down. The clock ticks. The fire pops. Prudence folds her hands over the ledger, a little tighter.

PRUDENCE
Orchids need potting, Inspector.

LANGLEY
So they do.

He lifts the bronze statue and weighs it in his palm.

LANGLEY (CONT'D)
Lady Whitmore. I think we should begin again.
